## Report
Validated the finding as a semantic/security regression, not a memory-safety crash. Code review showed the underlying authenticated Raft admin client intentionally preserves maybe-applied semantics: crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:13577-13581, 13601-13605, and 13623-13627 convert response-loss-after-dispatch into unconfirmed Raft admin errors, and crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:15134-15138 formats the warning with `may have applied` and `confirmation predicate`. The new facade wraps all source errors for leadership transfer, snapshot/purge, and election at crates/storage/src/control_plane_operator_admin.rs:49-50, 65-66, and 77-78. Its public Display/Error path then suppresses the source: crates/storage/src/control_plane_operator_admin.rs:256-260 renders any Source as only `control-plane <operation> failed`, and crates/storage/src/control_plane_operator_admin.rs:268 implements Error without exposing `source()`. The CLI-facing helpers convert that redacted error to String at crates/argmin-s3/src/main.rs:1026-1043. Dynamic PoC: I added a targeted storage unit test that accepts an authenticated TransferRaftLeadership RPC, applies it to the test authority, then drops the Unix stream before a response. Running `cargo test -p storage operator_raft_admin_facade_redacts_unconfirmed_after_lost_response -- --nocapture` produced: `operator display after maybe-applied response loss: control-plane Raft leadership transfer failed` and `authority applied leadership transfers: [102]`. This demonstrates the operation may have applied, but the operator-visible error has lost the unconfirmed-operation warning. Existing targeted tests also passed: `authenticated_raft_admin_triggers_fail_unconfirmed_after_lost_response` confirms the underlying unconfirmed classification, and `operator_error_retains_source_without_exposing_it` confirms Display/debug/source redaction. Crash/ASan/Valgrind attempts: a direct crash was not expected because this is an error-semantics issue; the PoC test completed without a process crash. Valgrind was unavailable (`bash: command not found: valgrind`). Rust ASan could not be used because the stable toolchain rejects `-Z sanitizer=address`. I then used LLDB non-interactively and set a breakpoint on `ControlPlaneOperatorAdminError` Display; the backtrace stopped at crates/storage/src/control_plane_operator_admin.rs:258 from `ToString::to_string`, confirming the CLI/String path enters the redacting formatter.

